WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.460
The ending of "Moon Knight" does some heavy
lifting to wrap up the season's story – and

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:08.534
then at the last minute, it seriously pulls
the rug out from under the viewers' feet.

00:00:08.534 --> 00:00:12.220
Warning - spoilers for "Moon Knight" Episode
6 ahead.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:15.141
"Marc Spector…

00:00:15.141 --> 00:00:18.110
Steven Grant…

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:20.810
Whoever else might be in there."

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:24.840
As Arthur Harrow notes in "Moon Knight," the
head of Marc Spector-slash-Steven Grant is

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.620
more than a little crowded.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:30.970
By the time the finale episode is over, this
umpteenth little tease about another personality's

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:33.929
existence comes into play in a major way.

00:00:33.929 --> 00:00:38.129
Despite the warnings of Taweret, Marc Spector
turns his back to the peaceful Field of Reeds,

00:00:38.129 --> 00:00:39.989
and chooses to rescue Steven from Duat.

00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:43.660
He still finds a form of peace, though, after
finally confessing that he needs Steven to

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:48.030
keep together, and even calling the cheerful
Brit identity his only true superpower — a

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:52.249
pretty heavy statement from a guy who wields
powers granted by a moon god.

00:00:52.249 --> 00:00:57.670
With that, Marc and Steven become whole, return to their body, and become Moon Knight (and Mr. Knight) again.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:01.039
Unfortunately, even their combined forces
aren't quite enough to defeat Harrow, who

00:01:01.039 --> 00:01:03.309
comes within inches of killing Marc.

00:01:03.309 --> 00:01:08.280
However, after Marc experiences another unexplained
mid-fight blackout, it's Harrow who lies helpless

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:09.640
on the ground.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:13.840
At this point, even the most casual viewer
probably suspects that a third, unseen identity

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.219
is responsible for knocking Harrow's glass-filled
shoes off his feet.

00:01:17.219 --> 00:01:21.340
After all, the show has teased this dangerous
persona's existence since Episode 3.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:23.780
"Steven, what did you do?"

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:25.000
"I swear that wasn't me."

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.130
"Then who was it?"

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:32.590
By the time the post-credits scene comes along,
the Jake Lockley identity indeed makes his presence known.

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:36.609
Wearing his signature flat cap, the violent
Jake hijacks Harrow from the hospital, and

00:01:36.609 --> 00:01:38.820
shoots him while Khonshu looks on.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:44.030
In this short scene, this new persona establishes
himself as a ruthless and capable operator.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:47.860
What's worse, he's still willingly working
with Khonshu, and since he occupies the same

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:52.210
body as Steven and Marc, it appears that none
of them are as free from the lunar god's servitude

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:53.473
as they think.

00:01:53.473 --> 00:01:56.810
"Moon Knight" has been teasing the possibility
that Layla El-Faouly might become the new

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:01.509
avatar of Khonshu for a while, and while she
does gain avatar-based superpowers in Episode

00:02:01.509 --> 00:02:04.380
6, this happens in a very different way than
you might think.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:08.660
Sure, the show teases a "Layla as Moon Knight"
moment when she releases Khonshu from his

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:13.290
mini-statue prison and the moon god offers
to make her his new avatar — but Layla promptly

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:18.230
refuses, and even attempts to browbeat Khonshu
into partnership instead of a servitude deal.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:21.910
This doesn't go particularly well, but as
it turns out, this isn't the first time Layla

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:25.230
cold-shoulders an Egyptian god's avatar offer
that day.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:28.905
"Only an avatar can do it."

00:02:28.905 --> 00:02:31.510
"I said, 'no.'"

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:35.670
Earlier in the episode, Taweret delivers Marc's
message to Layla, and throws in a far less

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:38.030
ominous avatar deal in the process.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:42.810
Wary of Marc's negative experiences, Layla
initially turns Taweret down, but reconsiders

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:47.900
upon learning that Team Egyptian Pantheon
needs more avatars to successfully trap Ammit.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.620
As such, she accepts the hippo goddess' offer.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:54.080
This proves to be a good call, because the
delighted Taweret provides Layla with some

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:59.040
welcome information about her father's afterlife
situation, and grants her a cool, winged costume.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.770
"Wow, you look amazing.

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:02.500
What are you wearing?"

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:05.480
Layla appears to highly enjoy her new avatar
powers.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:09.170
What's more, the show pretty much states that
she's not going away any time soon, when a

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:12.890
young girl asks her whether she's an Egyptian
superhero, and Layla immediately confirms

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:14.290
that she is.

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:17.320
Just like that, "Moon Knight" has delivered
the origin story of a character who's been

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:21.393
confirmed to be the Scarlet Scarab, making
her debut in the MCU.

00:03:21.393 --> 00:03:25.120
Harrow continues his penance-filled path in
this episode of "Moon Knight," and his mission

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.760
to let Ammit clean the world keeps adding
to the show's body count.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:32.390
After pulling assorted "don't resist and you
don't get hurt" stunts — including one on

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:36.710
the Egyptian gods' avatars — he finally
releases the crocodile-headed judgment deity,

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:39.130
who promptly makes Harrow her official avatar.

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:43.090
From this point on, things play out much like
Harrow promised in Episode 1.

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:47.680
Ammit's followers start cleansing the world, and the goddess feasts on the souls the scales deem unworthy.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.260
Unfortunately for the villains, the plan is
rudely interrupted by the freshly resurrected

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:55.460
Moon Knight and the Tawaret-powered Layla,
who manage to defeat Harrow and trap Ammit

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:56.530
in his body.

00:03:56.530 --> 00:04:01.760
The shock is enough to hospitalize Harrow,
but even then, he finds no peace despite Ammit's promises.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:06.880
Instead, Jake Lockley unceremoniously abducts
and shoots him, destroying Ammit in the process.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:10.960
It's not exactly a surprise that the villain
loses in the end, but the way Episode 6 presents

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:15.230
Harrow as a vulnerable, hurt, and deeply regretful
man who's simply going through his twisted

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:19.430
perception of doing the right thing is a pretty
nice take for a MCU antagonist.

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:23.100
By the time he's forced to act as Ammit's
living, broken vessel, Harrow has clearly

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:27.370
endured so much pain that even the hitherto
implacable Doctor Harrow construct in Marc's

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:29.323
mind is hurt and terrified.

00:04:29.323 --> 00:04:33.940
Few MCU properties can escape the big CGI
ending battle, so by the time Harrow stands

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:37.460
on a pyramid and Moon Knight is flying around,
it's clear that "Moon Knight" is about to

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:39.190
deliver on this front.

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:42.060
Instead of the Harrow-Moon Knight showdown
that you might have been expecting, though,

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.740
the show turns this ultimate battle into a
full-on superteam fight.

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:49.470
Moon Knight keeps alternating between his
main costume and Steven's Mr. Knight persona,

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:54.433
the freshly avatarized Layla soon turns up
to help, and Harrow's cultists keep stirring the pot.

00:04:54.433 --> 00:04:59.274
In the background, Ammit and Khonshu keep
themselves busy with a kaiju-sized grudge match.

00:04:59.274 --> 00:05:01.270
This goes pretty much as you'd expect.

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:04.720
After almost getting defeated, the good guys
ultimately win the night, and even Steven

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:06.700
gets to demonstrate some sweet moves.

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:10.710
However, this being "Moon Knight," the real
battle is just beginning ... and, ultimately,

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:12.070
inside Marc's head.

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:16.000
As the post-credits scene reveals, Khonshu's
taken to deploying Marc's third personality,

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.310
Jake, as his secret avatar, which effectively
renders his release of Marc and Steven completely

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:22.620
moot as they share the same body.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:26.680
And so, like cycles of the moon, the protagonist
ends where he began — in Steven's messy

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:30.860
London apartment, with the confused main character's
leg chained to his bed in a futile attempt

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.473
to stop blackout-walking, and the same song
playing in the background.

00:05:34.473 --> 00:05:38.893
Meanwhile, a mysterious, violent personality
is making moves out in the world.

00:05:38.893 --> 00:05:42.940
"Moon Knight" doesn't shy away from the fact
that Khonshu isn't the nicest or most effective

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:44.370
deity around.

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:48.260
As everyone from Harrow to Layla is quick
to point out, he's an abusive and treacherous

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:52.480
entity at the best of times, and Khonshu himself
is quick to confirm all the negative reviews

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:56.410
with his condescending attitude, overly theatrical
delivery, and rampant name-calling.

00:05:56.410 --> 00:06:00.550
It doesn't exactly help the moon god's case
that he manages to get himself trapped in

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:05.316
a statue halfway through the show, and starts
throwing shade at Layla the second she releases him.

00:06:05.316 --> 00:06:08.990
The end of "Moon Knight" Episode 6, however,
features copious Khonshu action, and makes

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:12.290
it clear that the deity is far more than a
pompous poser.

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:16.400
Not only does he fearlessly engage the dangerous
Ammit in combat on two separate occasions,

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:21.000
but he also reveals himself as the kind of
ruthless trickster that Loki would be proud of.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:25.240
His interest in Layla as a potential future
avatar was a feint all along, and the moon

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:28.770
god has actually been working with Marc's
Jake Lockley identity to ensure that he stays

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:29.980
in Khonshu's service.

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:34.740
Though Khonshu is technically fighting evil,
it certainly seems that he's no angel himself,

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.030
and may actually be outright dangerous.

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:41.520
He's extremely dedicated to keeping Marc-Steven-Jake
as his avatar, and his final scene in the

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:44.800
episode hints that this obsession is about
to turn hostile.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:48.580
Not only does he outright declare that Marc's
troubles have only just begun, but his new,

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:52.410
sweet suit resembles the outfit he wears in
the Jeff Lemire-penned "Moon Knight" comics

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:54.520
... where Khonshu is the villain.

